WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.850
My phone slipped into a

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.330
bucket of water at break.

3
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:06.850
Three years of class photos, my

4
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.710
marks, every parent’s number.

5
00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.260
Is it all gone?

6
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.680
Today: how to lose a phone,

7
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:14.860
but not your work.

8
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.420
Phones get lost, stolen

9
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.300
and dropped in water.

10
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.980
Laptops die, often the week before exams.

11
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.570
And ransomware is malware that locks your

12
00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.950
files and asks for money to open them.

13
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.240
A backup is a second copy, kept

14
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.840
somewhere else, so one bad day

15
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.200
doesn’t take everything.

16
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.610
Professionals use the three-two-one rule.

17
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.110
Three copies of what matters.

18
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.570
On two different kinds of storage,

19
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.850
like your phone and the cloud.

20
00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:53.090
And one copy away from school, so a fire

21
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:54.750
or a flood can’t take them all.

22
00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.130
So what matters?

23
00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.530
Marks and registers.

24
00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.730
Lesson plans and exam papers.

25
00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.550
Class photos, and your contacts,

26
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.370
with every parent’s number.

27
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.980
Turn on your phone’s automatic backup,

28
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.640
and your chat app’s backup, so it

29
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.500
happens every night without you.

30
00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.810
Careful: syncing isn’t the

31
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.590
same as a backup.

32
00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.660
If a file is deleted or locked on

33
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.980
your phone, the synced copy can be

34
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:25.400
deleted or locked too.

35
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.410
Version history helps, because you

36
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.870
can go back to yesterday’s copy.

37
00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.750
But one copy should always sit unplugged,

38
00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:36.630
on a USB drive or an external disk.

39
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:38.340
Quick check.

40
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.540
Ransomware locks the

41
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.640
school office computer.

42
00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.590
There’s a USB drive still plugged

43
00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.310
in, and another that was unplugged

44
00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.390
after Friday’s backup.

45
00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.180
Which copy saves you?

46
00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.010
The one unplugged after Friday.

47
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.810
Ransomware locks everything it can

48
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.490
reach, so the plugged-in drive and the

49
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.290
synced folder are locked too.

50
00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.810
Report it, don’t pay, and restore

51
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.950
Friday’s copy onto a clean computer.

52
00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:08.560
A backup you’ve never

53
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.360
opened is only a hope.

54
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.690
Once a term, open one file from your

55
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.430
backup and check it’s all there.

56
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.140
Then you know it works

57
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.640
before the bad day comes.

58
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.770
How does a computer know a copy is exact?

59
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.020
It makes a fingerprint of

60
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.800
each file, called a hash.

61
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:29.410
Change one letter, and the

62
00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.270
fingerprint changes completely.

63
00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.690
You’ll make some yourself in the lab.

64
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:35.480
Your turn.

65
00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.750
I keep my marks on my phone,

66
00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.290
with a copy in the cloud.

67
00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.760
Do I meet the three-two-one rule?

68
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.390
Not yet: that’s only two copies.

69
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.040
Add a third, on a USB drive kept at home.

70
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.350
Remember: three copies, two kinds of

71
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:55.330
storage, one away from school, one

72
00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.510
unplugged, and a test restore every term.

73
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.800
Try it in class tomorrow: tell

74
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.700
the story of a lost phone.

75
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.540
Groups draw where three copies of the

76
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.040
class register could live, and circle the

77
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.480
one that would survive a flood.
